@implements IDisposable
@inject BacklogDesktopState State
@inject GitHubIntegration GitHub
@inject IAppFeatureSettings Features
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@*
    A list of entries on the left and the selected entry on the right — the shape
    Microsoft To Do settled on, built out of the controls the storybook already
    documents rather than a second set living here.

    The list is TaskListView. Which row is open is the pane's to say and not the
    row's, so it arrives as SelectedId; completing, renaming and re-ranking are
    the list's three callbacks and the writes behind them are unchanged.

    The detail pane is where every control over one entry now lives: its title
    row, its body, the five scheduling and dependency rows, the selectors, and —
    behind Ctrl+Shift+M — the canonical markdown itself. That last one
    is the escape hatch `.design/content-editing.md#raw-markdown-escape-hatch`
    asks for, and it is deliberately no longer the primary mode: `#editing-model`
    says edit-in-place, "not a split write-raw/preview as the primary mode", and
    this pane had that inverted.

    The body has two readings and shows one at a time: the steps, or the markdown
    block they are written in. They are the same characters — `EntryTextParser`
    reads a `##` chapter as a step — so switching is a view change and nothing is
    converted. Which one an entry opens in is remembered on the entry itself, as a
    `view:` token, because the markdown is canonical and a preference kept beside
    the file would not survive the file being shared. The steps view says so when
    it is leaving prose off the screen rather than quietly dropping it.

    The panel is the fixed half of the split and the list flexes, which is the
    opposite of how it started. See the comment on the SplitPane below.

    The shell decides whether the pane is on screen and nothing more.

    Selection is what puts the detail pane on screen, and losing the focus is what
    takes it off again: a focusout that lands outside the detail half — or its
    own separator, which sits between the two rather than inside either — is the
    reader having moved on. The listener still sits on the section wrapping both
    halves, because that is where every focusout from either side bubbles to;
    what changed is what "outside" means to the check itself. The check is a
    question asked of the document rather than of the event, because Blazor's
    FocusEventArgs carries no relatedTarget — see `backlogFocusOutside`.

    A row picked while that check is still resolving is not a "did they leave"
    the answer arrived late for — it is a different entry replacing the one the
    focusout was ever about, and closing on top of it would hide the row the
    reader just opened. See the guard in OnPaneFocusOutAsync.
*@

<section @key=@("backlog-pane") class="backlog-workspace" id="backlog-pane" data-testid="backlog-pane" aria-label="Backlog entries" @onfocusout="OnPaneFocusOutAsync">

    @* The separator drags now, which it did not before: the shared resizer kept a
       single owner for the whole document and the desktop shell claims it for the
       knowledge panel, so this split had to opt out of the pointer gesture and
       keep only its keyboard resize — a handle you could tab to and not drag. The
       resizer keys its owners by layout, so this one is its own.

       The split stays mounted with nothing selected and collapses to one column
       through a modifier class instead. Swapping it for a bare list would rebuild
       the list: the scroll position and the focused row would both go, which is
       precisely what the reader was in the middle of using. *@
    @* Anchored to the end, so the panel is the fixed half and the list takes
       everything it does not need. It was the other way round and the arithmetic
       said so: a fixed 480px list beside a panel that took the rest gave the panel
       846px of a 1400px window — nearly twice the column the reader actually scans.
       The list is the surface you work in; the panel shows one entry, and one entry
       does not get wider the wider the window is. *@
    <SplitPane CssClass="@SplitCssClass"
               TestId="backlog-split"
               SeparatorTestId="backlog-split-separator"
               AriaLabel="Open entry width"
               Anchor="SplitPaneAnchor.End"
               FixedWidthRem="@_detailWidthRem"
               FixedWidthRemChanged="rem => _detailWidthRem = rem"
               MinRem="24">
        <Start>
            <div class="backlog-list">
                @* Areas are local piles/tags inside the selected repository scope.
                   Status narrows what is already in view, so it sits on the right. *@
                <div class="filter-bar">
                    @* My Day is a scope rather than one of a set, so it holds a
                       state of its own: it narrows whatever area and status have
                       already left in view instead of replacing either. That is
                       what makes it a ToggleButton with aria-pressed beside two
                       radiogroups — the difference is in the semantics, not just
                       in the shape. *@
                    <div class="filter-group filter-group--scope" role="group" aria-label="Scope">
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="chip chip--filter chip--scope"
                                      PressedCssClass="chip--active"
                                      Pressed="@MyDayOnly"
                                      TestId="myday-filter-option"
                                      Title="@(MyDayOnly ? "Show everything again" : "Show only what is in today's My Day")"
                                      PressedChanged="OnMyDayFilterChanged">
                            My Day<span class="chip__count">@MyDayCount</span>
                        </ToggleButton>
                    </div>

                    <div class="filter-group filter-group--areas" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Filter by area">
                        @foreach (var area in State.AreaFilters)
                        {
                            @* A radio in a radiogroup, so aria-checked rather than the
                               aria-pressed ToggleButton would bring: these chips pick one
                               of a set, they do not each hold a state of their own. *@
                            <AppButton BaseClass="chip chip--filter"
                                       Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                                       CssClass="@(State.SelectedArea == area.Value ? "chip--active" : null)"
                                       TestId="area-filter-option"
                                       role="radio"
                                       aria-checked="@(State.SelectedArea == area.Value ? "true" : "false")"
                                       OnClick="() => State.SetAreaFilter(area.Value)">
                                @area.Label<span class="chip__count">@area.Count</span>
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                    </div>

                    <div class="filter-group filter-group--status" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Filter by status">
                        @foreach (var filter in State.StatusFilters)
                        {
                            <AppButton BaseClass="chip chip--filter"
                                       Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                                       CssClass="@(State.SelectedStatusFilterWire == filter.Wire ? "chip--active" : null)"
                                       id="@($"status-filter-{(string.IsNullOrEmpty(filter.Wire) ? "all" : filter.Wire)}")"
                                       TestId="status-filter-option"
                                       role="radio"
                                       aria-checked="@(State.SelectedStatusFilterWire == filter.Wire ? "true" : "false")"
                                       OnClick="() => OnFilterChanged(filter.Wire)">
                                @filter.Label
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>

                @if (State.FilteredRows.Count == 0)
                {
                    @* "You have written nothing" and "your filters match nothing" are
                       different facts, and the second one became common the moment My
                       Day arrived: most entries are not in today's My Day, so the
                       first-use copy would tell somebody with a full backlog to write
                       their first entry. No CTA on the filtered one — the chips that
                       caused it are the line above, and a button here would duplicate
                       a control already on screen. *@
                    @if (State.ScopedRows.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <EmptyState Title="Nothing here yet."
                                    Description="Write your first entry to start triaging the backlog."
                                    data-testid="empty-state" />
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <EmptyState Title="Nothing matches these filters."
                                    Description="There are entries here — none of them are in what the bar above is asking for."
                                    data-testid="empty-state" />
                    }

                    @* With no rows there is no list for the slot below to be a slot
                       in, and the control that writes the first entry is the one
                       thing on this half that still has to be reachable. *@
                    @RenderAddEntry()
                }
                else
                {
                    @* Copying is on, and what it hands over is this pane's answer
                       rather than the row's. An entry row carries no Body on purpose —
                       see EntryTasks — so a copy left to the shared row's default would
                       be a bare title, which is not a brief anyone can paste.

                       The taste argument this used to refuse on — a clipboard button on
                       every row being the loudest thing in a column whose job is
                       scanning — is answered where it belongs, in the row's own CSS: the
                       button sits in the disabled-text colour and only reaches full
                       contrast once the row is hovered or the button focused. Quiet, but
                       present, because an affordance that appears on hover cannot be
                       found by touch or by keyboard. *@
                    <TaskListView @ref="_list"
                                  Tasks="@EntryTasks()"
                                  SelectedId="@SelectedTaskId"
                                  OnSelectedTabForward="MoveFocusToDetailAsync"
                                  OnSelected="OnEntrySelectedAsync"
                                  OnToggle="OnEntryToggledAsync"
                                  OnRename="OnEntryRenamedAsync"
                                  Reorderable="true"
                                  OnReorder="OnEntryReorderedAsync"
                                  GroupCompleted="true"
                                  RowCopyValue="@EntryCopyText"
                                  RowCssClass="@RepositoryMark"
                                  AriaLabel="Backlog entries"
                                  TestId="entry-list">
                        <RowActions Context="task">
                            @* The two edits a reader makes while scanning rather than
                               while reading one entry: what this is filed against, and
                               how far it has got. Both were on the row already — the
                               repository as the stripe down its edge, the status as a
                               badge beside the title — and neither could be changed
                               without opening the entry first.

                               A row the list is drawing is always a row the pane has,
                               so the resolve back to the entry is total in practice;
                               the branch is here because RowActions is handed a
                               TaskRow and a TaskRow is a task, with no area, no
                               repository and no status vocabulary on it. *@
                            @if (EntryFor(task.Id) is { } entry)
                            {
                                @RenderRowPickers(entry, task.Title)
                            }
                        </RowActions>
                        <AfterOpenRows>
                            @RenderAddEntry()
                        </AfterOpenRows>
                    </TaskListView>
                }
            </div>
        </Start>

        <End>
            @* Nothing at all with nothing selected, rather than a placeholder in a
               half the list could be using. A hidden pane whose contents were still
               being built would also still be a pane: every control in it would be
               rendered, kept in sync, and tabbable-in-principle for the sake of
               something no reader can see. *@
            @if (State.SelectedRow is { } selected)
            {
                @RenderDetail(selected)
            }
        </End>
    </SplitPane>

    @* What the last import produced, once the dialog it ran from has closed —
       the same "reads as" sentence a single save already gives, for a whole
       batch. Dismissable, the same as every other transient result this pane
       reports; not auto-dismissed, because "3 created, 1 updated" is exactly the
       kind of line somebody opened the dialog to go and check against the list. *@
    <Alert Message="@_importMessage" BaseClass="import-plan-result" Role="status" TestId="import-plan-result" />
</section>

<ImportPlanDialog @ref="_importDialog"
                   Open="@_importOpen"
                   OpenChanged="OnImportOpenChanged"
                   Busy="@_importBusy"
                   Options="@RepositoryOptions()"
                   OnImport="OnImportAsync" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// How wide the open entry's panel is, in rem. The list takes the rest.
    /// <para>
    /// 36rem, which is the width the panel needs rather than the least it will
    /// survive. It opened at 24rem — the floor <c>BACKLOG_PANE_MIN_REM</c> and
    /// <c>SplitPane.MinRem</c> agree on — on the theory that a reader only ever
    /// drags a panel wider. What that actually did was open every entry in the one
    /// shape the detail pane is worst in: 24rem leaves about 22rem of content,
    /// under the pane's 16rem column width doubled, so the groups came out in a
    /// single stack and the classification strip wrapped into stranded lines. The
    /// pane's own storybook reviews it at 44rem for the same reason.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// 40rem, and the number is arithmetic rather than taste. The detail pane's
    /// columns are <c>16rem</c> wide with a <c>--spacing-md</c> gutter, so two of
    /// them need 33rem of content; the panel spends <c>--spacing-lg</c> of padding
    /// on each side and a border. 36rem was tried first and came out 2px short of
    /// the second column — a single stack on every entry, which is exactly the
    /// shape the width was raised to avoid. 40rem clears it by about 60px, which is
    /// the margin that keeps it two columns rather than two columns on this
    /// machine.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// On a 1400px window that leaves the list about 47rem, still wider than the
    /// 480px column it had when the list was the fixed half. The floor stays 24rem,
    /// so the old width is still reachable — it is just no longer the one a reader
    /// is given.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Held here rather than handed over as a literal, because the split keeps the
    /// width the separator gave it in a parameter: a literal is re-supplied on every
    /// render, and this pane renders on every state change and now on every focus
    /// move as well. A keyboard resize would have lasted until the next one of
    /// those.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private double _detailWidthRem = 40;

    /// <summary>The list, held so the focus can be put back on the open row from
    /// the pane beside it. The list names its own row elements, so asking it is the
    /// only honest way to reach one.</summary>
    private TaskListView? _list;

    /// <summary>The open entry's panel, held for the same reason and in the other
    /// direction: it is where the focus goes when the reader tabs off the selected
    /// row. The panel names its own controls, so it is asked to focus itself rather
    /// than handed an id to put on one — unlike a row in a list, it is one instance
    /// this pane already holds.</summary>
    private TaskPanel? _panel;

    /// <summary>The detail half on its own — what a focus leaving now means,
    /// rather than the section holding both halves. Matches the <c>id</c> below.</summary>
    private const string DetailElementId = "backlog-pane-detail";

    /// <summary>The two regions a focusout is not a departure from: the detail
    /// pane itself, and the split's own separator, named by its stable
    /// data-testid rather than a second id since nothing else needs to address it
    /// directly. Selectors rather than plain ids because the separator has no id
    /// of its own to match — see <see cref="OnPaneFocusOutAsync"/>.</summary>
    private static readonly string[] DetailBoundarySelectors =
    [
        $"#{DetailElementId}",
        "[data-testid='backlog-split-separator']"
    ];

    /// <summary>Which of the selected entry's scheduling rows currently has its
    /// picker open. One per pane rather than one per row: two open pickers is a
    /// question about which of them the keyboard belongs to that nobody
    /// asked.</summary>
    private SchedulePicker? _openPicker;

    private enum SchedulePicker
    {
        Due,
        Reminder,
        Repeat,
        DependsOn,
        Priority,
        Effort,
        Attachment
    }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
        Features.Changed += HandleFeaturesChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>The raw editor's element id, so the caret can be put in it the render
    /// after it appears. Fixed rather than per row: there is only ever one open, and
    /// the shortcut has to be able to name it without knowing which entry is
    /// selected.</summary>
    private const string RawEditorId = "entry-raw-editor";

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the caret wherever the last state change said it was owed, the render
    /// after the control that takes it exists.
    /// <para>
    /// Two destinations, and they arrive as two values rather than one flag with a
    /// condition on it. The hatch wants it because otherwise Ctrl+Shift+M reveals a
    /// textarea and leaves the focus on the pane, so the shortcut's whole point —
    /// reaching the source without hunting for a control — costs a click anyway. A
    /// brand-new entry wants it in its title, which is the field it cannot be saved
    /// without and the one the panel would never land on by itself: the panel's
    /// first control is its done circle.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The hatch is named by id, because the element does not exist until this
    /// render and there is nothing to hold an ElementReference to when the toggle is
    /// pressed. The title is not named at all — the panel is asked to open its own
    /// field, so which element that is stays the panel's answer, exactly as it is
    /// for the Tab hand-off above.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The intent is only cleared once it has been acted on. An intent whose
    /// element is not on screen yet is still owed, and consuming it here would drop
    /// the caret on the floor.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        switch (State.PendingCaret)
        {
            case PendingCaret.RawMarkdown when State.RawHatchOpen:
                State.PendingCaret = PendingCaret.None;
                await FocusRawEditorAsync();
                return;

            case PendingCaret.EntryTitle when _panel is not null:
                State.PendingCaret = PendingCaret.None;
                await _panel.EditTitleAsync();
                return;
        }
    }

    private async Task FocusRawEditorAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", RawEditorId);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // The box is there and typeable; it just did not take focus. Losing the
            // caret is not worth tearing down the page.
        }
    }

    private void HandleStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void HandleFeaturesChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private bool IsFeatureEnabled(string featureKey) => Features.IsEnabled(featureKey);

    private bool AdditionalRepositoriesEnabled => IsFeatureEnabled(BacklogFeatures.AdditionalRepositories);

    /// <summary>The repositories an entry may be filed against. Without the
    /// additional-repositories feature there is only ever the first one.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<GitHubRepositoryRef> KnowledgeRepositories
    {
        get
        {
            if (AdditionalRepositoriesEnabled) return GitHub.Repositories;

            var repository = GitHub.Repositories.FirstOrDefault();
            return repository is null ? [] : [repository];
        }
    }

    private GitHubRepositoryRef? RepositoryFor(EntryRow row) =>
        AdditionalRepositoriesEnabled ? State.RepositoryFor(row) : GitHub.Repositories.FirstOrDefault();

    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> RepositoryOptions() =>
        [.. KnowledgeRepositories.Select(repository => new SelectorOption(repository.Alias, repository.Alias))];

    /// <summary>The split's classes, and with nothing selected the modifier that
    /// gives the list the whole width. A class rather than a different component:
    /// see the comment on the element itself.</summary>
    private string SplitCssClass =>
        State.SelectedRow is null ? "backlog-split backlog-split--solo" : "backlog-split";

    // --- The list ----------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// What a row is called in the list's own vocabulary.
    /// <para>
    /// The persisted id when there is one, because that is what an
    /// <c>after:</c> token names and the list resolves dependencies by id. A row
    /// the store has never seen falls back to its client-side key: nothing can
    /// wait on it, so nothing will ever look it up.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string EntryTaskId(EntryRow row) => (row.Id ?? row.Key).ToString();

    private string? SelectedTaskId => State.SelectedRow is { } row ? EntryTaskId(row) : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// The filtered entries, as the shared list needs them.
    /// <para>
    /// Two fields are deliberately absent. <c>Important</c> — the star — has no
    /// field on a backlog entry, and setting it from something else would be
    /// inventing domain state the product has not decided on. <c>Body</c> is left
    /// off too: a body would give every row in the column a disclosure, and the
    /// note belongs in the pane beside the list rather than folded into the list
    /// twice. The note glyph still appears, because <c>Note</c> says there is more
    /// here than the title.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>Status</c> is absent too, and that is a move rather than a loss: the row
    /// carries a status <em>picker</em> now, at the end of it, and the read-only
    /// badge said the same word two elements to the left of a control that says it
    /// and can change it. Of the two, the one a reader's eye lands on first should
    /// not be the one they cannot act on.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> EntryTasks() =>
        [.. State.FilteredRows.Select(row => new TaskRow(
            EntryTaskId(row),
            string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(row.PreviewTitle) ? "Untitled" : row.PreviewTitle,
            Done: row.PreviewStatus is EntryStatus.Done,
            InMyDay: IsInMyDay(row),
            Group: EntryGroup(row),
            Due: FormatDate(row.PreviewDueOn),
            Reminder: FormatReminder(row.PreviewRemindAt),
            Repeats: row.PreviewRecurrence is not null,
            StepsDone: row.PreviewSubItems.Count(subItem => subItem.Done),
            StepCount: row.PreviewSubItems.Count,
            Note: row.HasExpandableContent,
            Tags: row.PreviewMetadataTags,
            DependsOn: row.PreviewDependsOn)
        {
            RepeatLabel = FormatRepeat(row.PreviewRecurrence)
        })];

    /// <summary>
    /// The area, for the quiet line under the title — unless the picker at the end
    /// of the row is already set to it.
    /// <para>
    /// An area is one field with two readings. Most of them are piles somebody
    /// typed, and nothing else on the row says "errands", so the line has to. Some
    /// of them name a configured repository, and then the repository picker is
    /// showing that alias two controls to the right — a metadata line repeating it
    /// would be the same word twice on a row whose width the title is already
    /// competing for.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Both halves of the test are needed, because the two <c>RepositoryFor</c>s
    /// are not the same question. <c>State</c>'s resolves the row's own area;
    /// the pane's is what the picker is set to, which with the
    /// additional-repositories feature off is the first configured repository no
    /// matter what the row says. Dropping the line on the pane's answer alone would
    /// hide "errands" behind a picker reading "backlog".
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? EntryGroup(EntryRow row) =>
        KnowledgeRepositories.Count > 0
        && State.RepositoryFor(row) is { } filed
        && RepositoryFor(row) is { } shown
        && string.Equals(filed.Alias, shown.Alias, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? null
            : row.PreviewArea;

    private EntryRow? EntryFor(string id) =>
        State.Rows.FirstOrDefault(row => string.Equals(EntryTaskId(row), id, StringComparison.Ordinal));

    /// <summary>
    /// What a row in the entry list copies, and what the panel it opens into
    /// copies with it: the entry's title, a blank line, and everything written
    /// under it.
    /// <para>
    /// The pane's answer rather than the row's, for the same reason
    /// <c>RepositoryMark</c> is: a <c>TaskRow</c> is a task and has no markdown on
    /// it. It has no <c>Body</c> either — <c>EntryTasks</c> leaves that off so the
    /// column does not grow a disclosure per row — so without this the copy would
    /// be a title on its own, which is a label rather than the brief somebody came
    /// to paste.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>ParsedEntry.Body</c> and not <c>GetNote</c>: the body runs to the end of
    /// the document and so brings every step with it. That is the point. A task
    /// whose steps were left behind is half a task, and the reader who copied it
    /// would find that out in the tool they pasted into.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The metadata line does not come along, because the parser has already read
    /// it off. `task` `*high` `!ready` is this app writing down what it knows about
    /// the entry, not text a person wrote, and it means nothing anywhere else.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Null for an entry with nothing under its title, rather than the title with
    /// two newlines welded on — that would copy a trailing blank line every time.
    /// The row reads null as "use the shared default", which is the title; the
    /// panel has no default to fall back to, so it is handed the title outright.
    /// Either way both surfaces copy the same thing.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? EntryCopyText(TaskRow task) =>
        EntryFor(task.Id) is { } entry ? EntryCopyText(entry) : null;

    /// <summary>The same answer for the panel, which has the entry in hand and so
    /// needs no resolve. One method for both surfaces on purpose: a reader who
    /// copies from the row and a reader who copies from the panel they opened it
    /// into must not get two different texts for the same task.</summary>
    private static string? EntryCopyText(EntryRow entry)
    {
        var body = EntryTextParser.Parse(entry.RawText).Body.Trim();

        return body.Length == 0 ? null : $"{DetailTitle(entry)}\n\n{body}";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The two pickers at the end of a list row: what the entry is filed against,
    /// and where it has got to.
    /// <para>
    /// In the row's action slot rather than beside the title, and that is settled by
    /// the markup rather than by taste: the status badge these replace was drawn
    /// <em>inside</em> <c>task-item__body</c>, which is a <c>button</c>, and a
    /// <c>select</c> cannot legally nest in one. The slot is where a host's own
    /// controls go — a fixed track at the end of the row, so the title is still the
    /// only thing that gives up width.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The slot already stops <c>click</c> and <c>mousedown</c>, so operating either
    /// picker neither opens the row nor starts a rename. Keys are covered by where
    /// the handlers are rather than by a third guard: the row's own key handling
    /// sits on the body button and the list's on its add field, and both are
    /// siblings of this span rather than ancestors of it. Drag is the one thing the
    /// slot does not already answer — the <c>li</c> is <c>draggable</c> and a
    /// <c>dragstart</c> raised inside it would be the row asking to be re-ranked —
    /// so the pointer is told there is nothing to pick up here and the event is
    /// stopped before it reaches the row that would act on it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The label on each names the entry, the way the row's own pencil does, because
    /// a column of these is otherwise one control announced twenty times.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderRowPickers(EntryRow row, string title) =>
        @<span class="entry-row__pickers" draggable="false" @ondragstart:stopPropagation="true">
            @if (KnowledgeRepositories.Count > 0)
            {
                @* The same list, the same resolve and the same write the panel's
                   picker uses, so the additional-repositories feature is honoured
                   here by construction rather than by a second reading of it. *@
                <RepositorySelector CurrentValue="@(RepositoryFor(row)?.Alias ?? string.Empty)"
                                    Options="@RepositoryOptions()"
                                    Label="@($"Change repository of {title}")"
                                    TestId="row-area-badge"
                                    OnChanged="value => OnRepositoryChangedAsync(row, value)" />
            }

            <StatusSelector CurrentValue="@row.PreviewStatus.ToString()"
                            Options="@StatusSelectorOptions()"
                            Label="@($"Change status of {title}")"
                            TestId="row-status-badge"
                            OnChanged="value => OnStatusChangedAsync(row, value)" />
        </span>;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which repository a row belongs to, as the identity mark's classes.
    /// <para>
    /// The pane's answer rather than the row's: <c>TaskRow</c> is a task and has no
    /// repository, which is why <c>TaskListView</c> asks for this per row instead of
    /// reading it off one. A row whose area names nothing configured gets no mark,
    /// which reads as "nobody said" rather than as one more project.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? RepositoryMark(TaskRow task) =>
        EntryFor(task.Id) is { } row ? State.RepositoryMarkClass(row) : null;

    private async Task OnEntrySelectedAsync(string id)
    {
        if (EntryFor(id) is not { } row) return;

        ClosePicker();
        await State.SelectAsync(row);
    }

    private async Task OnEntryToggledAsync(string id)
    {
        if (EntryFor(id) is { } row) await State.ToggleDoneAsync(row);
    }

    private async Task OnEntryRenamedAsync(TaskRename rename)
    {
        if (EntryFor(rename.Id) is { } row) await State.RenameEntryAsync(row, rename.Title);
    }

    private async Task OnEntryReorderedAsync(TaskMove move)
    {
        if (EntryFor(move.Id) is { } row && EntryFor(move.TargetId) is { } target)
        {
            await State.MoveEntryAsync(row, target);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Creating an entry is the same gesture as writing in one, so the control sits
    /// where the next entry will actually appear rather than up in the chrome.
    /// <para>
    /// Which is above the Completed section, and it took a slot on the list to get
    /// there: the list owns that section, so a button rendered after the whole
    /// component landed underneath a fold of finished work — the one place in the
    /// column where nothing new is ever going to show up. It is a fragment because
    /// it is drawn twice, once inside the list and once instead of it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderAddEntry() =>
        @<div class="entry-add-row">
            <AppButton BaseClass="entry-add"
                        Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                        id="new-entry-button"
                        TestId="new-entry-button"
                        OnClick="OnNewEntry">
                @* Not the Icon slot: that wraps what it is given in a `btn__icon`
                   span, and this row already has its own plus. *@
                <span class="entry-add__plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
                New entry
            </AppButton>
            @* Beside the entry it types in by hand, because both put entries in
               the same list the same way — a plan is faster typing, not a second
               way of holding work (ADR 0004). *@
            <AppButton BaseClass="entry-add entry-add--import"
                        Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                        id="import-plan-button"
                        TestId="import-plan-open"
                        OnClick="OnOpenImport">
                Import
            </AppButton>
        </div>;
    private void OnNewEntry() => State.NewRow();

    // --- Import --------------------------------------------------------

    private ImportPlanDialog? _importDialog;
    private bool _importOpen;
    private bool _importBusy;
    private string? _importMessage;

    private void OnOpenImport()
    {
        _importMessage = null;
        _importOpen = true;
        _importDialog?.OpenFresh();
    }

    private void OnImportOpenChanged(bool open) => _importOpen = open;

    /// <summary>
    /// Runs the import and reports what it produced, the same "reads as" sentence
    /// the raw hatch already gives for a single entry — the dialog shows it under
    /// the paste box on a refusal, and the pane's own alert repeats it after a
    /// successful run once the dialog has closed.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnImportAsync(ImportPlanSubmission submission)
    {
        _importBusy = true;
        try
        {
            var message = await State.ImportPlanAsync(submission.RawText, submission.DefaultRepo);

            // A validation failure ("nothing parsed to a title") is shown under
            // the paste box, where the text that caused it still is. A result
            // closes the dialog and moves the sentence to the pane, because the
            // pane — not the dialog the reader just dismissed — is where the new
            // rows now are.
            if (State.SaveState == AppSaveState.Error || message.StartsWith("Imported:", StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                _importOpen = false;
                _importMessage = message;
            }
            else
            {
                _importDialog?.SetError(message);
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _importBusy = false;
        }
    }

    private void OnFilterChanged(string wire) => State.SetStatusFilter(wire);

    // --- The My Day scope --------------------------------------------------

    private bool MyDayOnly => State.MyDayOn is not null;

    /// <summary>How many entries are in today's My Day, counted off the rows the
    /// repository scope leaves in view — the same pool the area chips count, so the
    /// number does not change meaning when a status is picked. Derived here because
    /// this is where the clock is.</summary>
    private int MyDayCount => State.ScopedRows.Count(IsInMyDay);

    /// <summary>Turning the scope on pins it to today. The date is read here rather
    /// than in the state for the reason <c>#feature-my-day</c> gives: membership is
    /// arithmetic against the reader's local date, and one place reads it.</summary>
    private void OnMyDayFilterChanged(bool pressed) => State.SetMyDayFilter(pressed ? Today : null);

    // --- The detail pane ---------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// One entry, as the shared side panel: the heading line, what it is filed
    /// under, the rows you set things on, its body, and — last — what acts on the
    /// whole entry.
    /// <para>
    /// The order is <c>TaskPanel</c>'s and not this pane's, which is the point of
    /// adopting it. What was here before was the same four regions in a slightly
    /// different order, drawn out of a <c>ul</c> of one row and a stack of divs;
    /// the panel says identity, classification, settings, content, and every
    /// screen that opens a task now says it the same way.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Each slot is filled with what the backlog specifically has, because that is
    /// the line the library draws: the panel says where a metadata strip goes and
    /// nothing about statuses, repositories or GitHub. <c>Status</c> is left unset
    /// for the same reason in reverse — the panel's is a word, and this entry's
    /// status is a picker, so it belongs in the strip with the other pickers.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The panel is also the pane's way in and out of the keyboard: it is asked to
    /// focus itself when the reader tabs off the selected row, and it reports the
    /// Shift+Tab that sends them back. Which control either of those lands on is
    /// the panel's business, which is why neither is named here.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderDetail(EntryRow row) =>
        @<div id="@DetailElementId" class="entry-detail" data-testid="entry-detail" @onkeydown="e => OnDetailKeyDownAsync(row, e)">
            <TaskPanel @ref="_panel"
                       Title="@DetailTitle(row)"
                       Done="@(row.PreviewStatus is EntryStatus.Done)"
                       OnToggle="() => State.ToggleDoneAsync(row)"
                       OnRename="title => State.RenameEntryAsync(row, title)"
                       OnTabBackward="MoveFocusToListAsync"
                       CopyValue="@(EntryCopyText(row) ?? DetailTitle(row))"
                       CssClass="entry-detail__panel"
                       TestId="entry-panel">
                <StatusContent>
                    @* On the heading line, where the list row also shows it. The
                       status was in the strip below with the other pickers, which
                       made a reader who opened a row look for it in a second place
                       — and it is the one thing in that strip that is a state
                       rather than a classification. *@
                    <StatusSelector CurrentValue="@row.PreviewStatus.ToString()"
                                    Options="@StatusSelectorOptions()"
                                    Label="Change status"
                                    TestId="status-badge"
                                    OnChanged="value => OnStatusChangedAsync(row, value)" />
                </StatusContent>
                <Filing>
                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(BacklogFeatures.GitHubIntegration))
                    {
                        <Alert Message="@row.GitHubError" BaseClass="entry-doc__gh-error" TestId="github-error" Role="status" />
                    }

                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatureKeys.CopilotCli))
                    {
                        <Alert Message="@row.CopilotError" BaseClass="entry-doc__gh-error" TestId="copilot-cli-error" Role="status" />
                    }

                    @RenderEntryMetadata(row)
                </Filing>
                <Details>
                    @RenderEntrySchedule(row)
                </Details>
                <Body>
                    @RenderBodyViews(row)
                </Body>
                <Footer>
                    @* The escape hatch. Reached from Ctrl+Shift+M on the pane, and
                       showing the exact canonical markdown that will be stored, in
                       mono, two-way, and auto-saving under the same rules as
                       everything else here.

                       There is no button for it any more. The row that used to open
                       it sat under the body reading "</> Markdown" while the body's
                       own view switch above already said "Markdown" — two controls,
                       one word, and only one of them the source. The switch is the
                       one a reader means nine times in ten, so the other one went
                       rather than being renamed into a distinction nobody was
                       looking for.

                       In the footer because it acts on the whole entry rather than
                       on one field of it, which is the same sentence that puts close
                       and delete beside it.

                       A brand-new entry does not get it either, and that is the one
                       fact this condition already carries: RawHatchOpen is the
                       selected row being the row with an editor open, and creating
                       an entry no longer opens one. A second condition here saying
                       "unless it is new" would be the same fact arriving twice. *@
                    @if (State.RawHatchOpen)
                    {
                        <div class="entry-detail__raw">
                            @* data-testid goes on the textarea rather than through
                               TestId, which lives on a wrapper Bare does not render —
                               the same way the date and reminder pickers are named. *@
                            <TextArea Bare="true"
                                      Id="@RawEditorId"
                                      AriaLabel="Entry markdown"
                                      Placeholder="@BacklogDesktopState.NewEntryPlaceholder"
                                      InputCssClass="entry-detail__raw-input"
                                      GrowCssClass="entry-detail__raw-grow"
                                      AutoGrow="true"
                                      Rows="8"
                                      data-testid="entry-raw-input"
                                      Value="@State.EntryEditText(row)"
                                      ValueChanged="value => State.OnRawTextInput(row, value)"
                                      @onblur="() => State.EndEditAsync(row)" />

                            @* Answers "which one is the status?" by simply saying so,
                               live, from the same parse the save will use. *@
                            <p class="entry-doc__reading" data-testid="entry-meta-reading">
                                <span class="entry-doc__reading-lead">reads as</span>
                                @foreach (var reading in row.MetaReadings)
                                {
                                    <span class="entry-doc__reading-item @(reading.Explicit ? string.Empty : "entry-doc__reading-item--default") @(reading.Note is null ? string.Empty : "entry-doc__reading-item--refused")">
                                        <span class="entry-doc__reading-kind">@reading.Kind</span>
                                        <span class="entry-doc__reading-value">@reading.Value</span>
                                        @if (reading.Note is not null)
                                        {
                                            <span class="entry-doc__reading-note">@reading.Note</span>
                                        }
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    }

                    <div class="entry-detail__footer">
                        @* Delete leads the row and close ends it, which is the same
                           arrangement the roadmap dialogs use: the destructive act at
                           the far end from everything else, never beside it. *@
                        <DeleteButton Lead="true"
                                      Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                                      id="@($"delete-entry-{row.Key}")"
                                      TestId="delete-entry-button"
                                      AriaLabel="Delete entry"
                                      OnClick="() => OnDeleteTaskAsync(row)" />

                        <IconButton BaseClass="entry-detail__close"
                                    TestId="close-entry-button"
                                    AriaLabel="Close this entry"
                                    OnClick="() => State.SelectAsync(null)">×</IconButton>
                    </div>
                </Footer>
            </TaskPanel>
        </div>;
    /// <summary>What the panel is called. An entry with no title line yet is
    /// "Untitled" rather than a blank heading, which is the same word the list
    /// puts on the same row — a panel that named it differently would look like a
    /// different entry.</summary>
    private static string DetailTitle(EntryRow row) =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(row.PreviewTitle) ? "Untitled" : row.PreviewTitle;

    // --- The body: steps, or the markdown block ----------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// The entry's body, in whichever of its two readings the reader asked for.
    /// <para>
    /// Two views of one text and no conversion between them. The steps are already
    /// parsed out of the body markdown by <c>EntryTextParser</c> — a <c>##</c>
    /// chapter is a step — so switching draws the same characters differently and
    /// writes them back the same way. A converter here would be a second source of
    /// truth with a migration in the middle of it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The choice is remembered on the entry, as a <c>view:</c> token on the metadata
    /// line (<c>.design/content-editing.md#scheduling-and-dependency-tokens</c>). Not
    /// because it is a fact about the work — it is a preference about looking at it —
    /// but because the markdown is canonical: a preference kept beside the file would
    /// not survive the file being shared.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderBodyViews(EntryRow row) =>
        @<div class="entry-detail__body">

            @* Tabs, not a pressed strip. Two readings of one text, one of them on
               screen at a time and the other reachable by name — that is a tab
               strip's job, and the strip carries the wiring a hand-rolled switch
               was only miming: `role="tablist"`, `aria-selected`, each panel
               labelled by its own tab, arrow keys between them, and one stop in
               the tab order for the pair rather than one per option.

               Bare, because this pane's body is already the column that places
               them. The wrapper and the panels box the library draws for a host
               without a layout of its own would be two more boxes to plumb the
               body's height through, for nothing on screen. *@
            <Tabs Bare="true"
                  ActiveId="@ViewId(row.EffectiveView)"
                  ActiveIdChanged="id => State.ChangeViewAsync(row, ViewFor(id))"
                  AriaLabel="Show the entry's body as"
                  ListCssClass="tabs__list entry-detail__views"
                  ListTestId="entry-view-switch">

                <TabPanel Id="@StepsViewId"
                          Title="Steps"
                          TabTestId="entry-view-steps"
                          TestId="entry-view-steps-panel"
                          BaseClass="entry-detail__view">
                    @* Steps, as their own list. Reordering, renaming and completing
                       are the same three callbacks the entry list uses.

                       DirectRename, so a step's title is a field without a pencil
                       first — and that is why the rows are still reorderable by
                       pointer only through their grip, and by keyboard through
                       Alt+Arrow inside the field. See TaskItem.

                       QuickEdit is the other half of that bargain, and it is also what
                       replaced the "next step" control that used to sit under this list.
                       Tab settles one step's title and opens the field on the next, and
                       off the bottom of the rows the list draws an add row of its own —
                       permanent, in the tab order after the last step, committing on
                       Enter or on leaving. A press that turned into a field, and a field
                       that then had to be dismissed, was a composer the reader had to
                       find twice; the shared one is simply there. AddLabel is what it is
                       called, to a screen reader and on the face of it: "Next", because
                       what the field asks for is the next step and the answer is the
                       only thing that ever gets typed into it.

                       Drawn whether or not the entry has chapters yet, which is the
                       point of adopting it: the count guard that used to stand in front
                       of this list also hid the only control that could create the first
                       step, so an entry with no steps had nothing here to press.

                       No EmptyMessage, so an entry with no steps draws the add row and
                       nothing above it. "No steps yet." was a line that said what the
                       empty field under it already showed, and two ways of saying
                       nothing is here is one more than the reader needs.

                       OnDelete is what puts a bin on the end of each step's row, and it
                       is the same deal as the rename: the list reports which step, this
                       pane decides what that means — here, the chapter and its notes cut
                       out of the entry's text. No confirmation, because the entry-level
                       bin in this pane's footer asks for none either and a step is the
                       cheaper of the two to lose. The lists in this pane that pass no
                       OnDelete — the entry list above, which deletes from the footer of
                       the open entry instead — grow no bin at all.

                       GroupCompleted is off: an entry's steps are a sequence
                       somebody wrote in an order, and moving the finished ones to
                       the bottom would rewrite that order on screen while the
                       markdown kept the real one.

                       Copying needs nothing said here, unlike the entry list above.
                       StepTasks already puts the step's note on the row as its Body,
                       so the shared row's own default — the title, a blank line, then
                       the body — is already the shape a step is worth pasting in.

                       Keyed on the entry, because the add row and an open rename hold
                       drafts of their own. Without a key this is one component for every
                       entry the reader opens, and a half-typed step would follow them to
                       the next one and be committed there — which is what the composer's
                       reset on selection used to prevent. *@
                    <TaskListView @key="row.Key"
                                  Tasks="@StepTasks(row)"
                                  OnToggle="index => OnStepToggledAsync(row, index)"
                                  OnRename="rename => OnStepRenamedAsync(row, rename)"
                                  DirectRename="true"
                                  QuickEdit="true"
                                  OnAdd="title => OnAddStepAsync(row, title)"
                                  AddLabel="Next"
                                  OnBodyChanged="change => OnStepNoteChanged(row, change)"
                                  OnDelete="index => OnDeleteStepAsync(row, index)"
                                  Reorderable="true"
                                  OnReorder="move => OnStepReorderedAsync(row, move)"
                                  GroupCompleted="false"
                                  BodyLabel="Notes"
                                  AriaLabel="Steps"
                                  EmptyMessage=""
                                  TestId="subitem-list" />

                    @if (row.StepsViewHidesProse)
                    {
                        @* Said out loud rather than hidden. The steps view lists
                           chapters, and the prose an entry opens with is not one — so
                           this view is showing less than the entry holds, and a reader
                           with no way to know that would read the missing paragraph as
                           text the app had lost. Quiet, one line, and the block that
                           does show it is the press at the end of the sentence. *@
                        <p class="entry-detail__elsewhere" data-testid="entry-view-elsewhere">
                            <span>There is text on this entry the steps do not show.</span>
                            <AppButton BaseClass="entry-detail__elsewhere-link"
                                       Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                                       TestId="entry-view-elsewhere-link"
                                       OnClick="() => State.ChangeViewAsync(row, EntryView.Notes)">
                                Show the markdown
                            </AppButton>
                        </p>
                    }
                </TabPanel>

                <TabPanel Id="@NotesViewId"
                          Title="Markdown"
                          TabTestId="entry-view-notes"
                          TestId="entry-view-notes-panel"
                          BaseClass="entry-detail__view">
                    @* The whole body, prose and chapters together. MarkdownEditor rather
                       than a textarea: this is prose written by the same person in the
                       same product as every other body, and the toolbar is the closest
                       this pane comes to the edit-in-place `#editing-model` asks for.
                       Not the note alone — a writer scoped to the prose half would
                       silently drop a `##` chapter typed into the block, and this block's
                       whole promise is that it is the text. *@
                    <MarkdownEditor Value="@EntryTextParser.Parse(row.RawText).Body"
                                    ValueChanged="body => State.ChangeBody(row, body)"
                                    AriaLabel="Markdown"
                                    ToolbarLabel="Formatting for the entry's markdown"
                                    Rows="6"
                                    AutoGrow="true"
                                    CssClass="entry-detail__note"
                                    TestId="entry-body-editor" />
                </TabPanel>
            </Tabs>
        </div>;

    private const string StepsViewId = "steps";
    private const string NotesViewId = "notes";

    /// <summary>Which tab is on, from the view the entry is being read in.</summary>
    private static string ViewId(EntryView view) =>
        view is EntryView.Steps ? StepsViewId : NotesViewId;

    /// <summary>And back again, when the reader presses the other tab.</summary>
    private static EntryView ViewFor(string id) =>
        string.Equals(id, StepsViewId, StringComparison.Ordinal) ? EntryView.Steps : EntryView.Notes;

    // --- Steps -------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// The entry's steps, indexed the way every sub-item write here is indexed —
    /// the nth <c>##</c> chapter. The id is the index, because a step has no
    /// identity of its own in the markdown: it is the chapter written in that
    /// position, which is also why moving one is a rewrite.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> StepTasks(EntryRow row) =>
        [.. Enumerable.Range(0, row.PreviewSubItems.Count).Select(index => new TaskRow(
            index.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture),
            EntryTextParser.GetSubItemTitle(row.RawText, index),
            Done: row.PreviewSubItems[index].Done,
            Body: EntryTextParser.GetSubItemNote(row.RawText, index) is { Length: > 0 } note ? note : null))];

    private static int StepIndex(string id) =>
        int.TryParse(id, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var index) ? index : -1;

    private async Task OnStepToggledAsync(EntryRow row, string id) =>
        await State.ToggleSubItemAsync(row, StepIndex(id));

    private async Task OnStepRenamedAsync(EntryRow row, TaskRename rename) =>
        await State.RenameSubItemAsync(row, StepIndex(rename.Id), rename.Title);

    private void OnStepNoteChanged(EntryRow row, TaskBodyChange change) =>
        State.ChangeSubItemNote(row, StepIndex(change.Id), change.Body);

    private async Task OnStepReorderedAsync(EntryRow row, TaskMove move) =>
        await State.MoveSubItemAsync(row, StepIndex(move.Id), StepIndex(move.TargetId));

    /// <summary>Deletes the step the bin was pressed on: the chapter, its own
    /// metadata line and its notes, cut out of the entry's text. The id is the
    /// index, exactly as it is for every other handler here — the step is the
    /// chapter written in that position, which is why deleting one renumbers the
    /// ones under it.</summary>
    private async Task OnDeleteStepAsync(EntryRow row, string id) =>
        await State.RemoveSubItemAsync(row, StepIndex(id));

    // A step used to carry two hand-off buttons of its own here — push this chapter
    // to GitHub as its own issue, and start Copilot CLI. Both are gone, and the
    // reason is in the model rather than in taste: `.domain/backlog/domain.md` gives
    // ProjectionRef to the entry and says a Sub-Item "may project to GitHub issue
    // task-list checkboxes", which are checkboxes inside the entry's issue. A step
    // that was its own issue had nowhere to record the link it got back, so it could
    // be pushed twice with nothing noticing — and the Copilot button on a step was
    // never about the step at all: it handed over the parent, from a row that made
    // it look otherwise. The entry-level pair is now labelled and grouped as acting
    // on the whole task, which is what it always did.

    /// <summary>
    /// A step, by title, as the steps list's own add row reports it.
    /// <para>
    /// The title is the whole of it, which is the deal <c>TaskListView.OnAdd</c>
    /// offers and the same one its rename already follows: the list says what was
    /// typed and this pane decides what it means — here, one more <c>##</c> chapter
    /// at the end of the entry's text. There is nothing to close afterwards, which
    /// is why this no longer resets anything: the field the title came from is a
    /// permanent row in the list rather than a state this pane was briefly in.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Trimmed and ignored when empty even though the list has already promised
    /// both. A write to the markdown is not the place to inherit somebody else's
    /// guarantee, and a chapter called nothing is one the reader cannot see, select
    /// or remove.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnAddStepAsync(EntryRow row, string title)
    {
        title = title.Trim();

        if (title.Length == 0) return;

        await State.AddSubItemAsync(row, title);
    }

    // --- Metadata ----------------------------------------------------------

    @* No stopPropagation here, and that is the point rather than an oversight. The
       badges used to sit inside a read view that opened the raw editor on click and
       on Enter, so every one of them had to keep both to itself or changing a status
       swapped the entry for a textarea mid-edit. There is no click-to-edit surface
       left: the pane is opened by selecting a row in the list, and the source is a
       toggle of its own. *@
    private RenderFragment RenderEntryMetadata(EntryRow row) =>
        @<div class="entry-doc__meta">
            <span class="entry-doc__meta-start">
                @* SelectField rather than EnumSelect: EnumSelect titles its own
                   labels ("In Progress"), and every other badge here reads
                   lowercase. The options come from here so the vocabulary stays
                   the app's. *@
                <SelectField CurrentValue="@row.PreviewType.ToString()"
                             Options="@TypeSelectorOptions()"
                             Label="Change type"
                             TestId="type-badge"
                             CssClass="metadata-editor--type badge badge--type"
                             OnChanged="value => OnTypeChangedAsync(row, value)" />

                @* Priority is not here. It is a row in the detail pane's Ranking
                   group, which is where the storybook puts it and where it stops
                   being read as one more label on a line of labels: how much
                   something matters is a decision, and this strip is where the
                   entry's classification is stated. *@

                @if (KnowledgeRepositories.Count > 0)
                {
                    <RepositorySelector CurrentValue="@(RepositoryFor(row)?.Alias ?? string.Empty)"
                                        Options="@RepositoryOptions()"
                                        Label="Change repository"
                                        TestId="area-badge"
                                        OnChanged="value => OnRepositoryChangedAsync(row, value)" />
                }

                @* Status is not here. It is on the panel's heading line, beside the
                   title, which is where the list row shows it too — a state is what
                   the title is currently doing, and this strip is for what the entry
                   is filed under. *@
            </span>

            <span class="entry-doc__meta-end">
                <span class="entry-doc__tags entry-doc__tags--end">
                    @RenderTagEditor(row)
                </span>

                @if (IsFeatureEnabled(BacklogFeatures.GitHubIntegration) && State.GitHubConfigured)
                {
                    @if (row.IssueLink is { } link)
                    {
                        <a class="badge badge--gh badge--gh-@(GitHubStateClass(row.Snapshot?.Issue.State))"
                           data-testid="github-issue-badge"
                           href="@link.Url"
                           target="_blank"
                           rel="noopener"
                           title="@link.RepoFullName issue @link.Label">
                            @link.Label
                            <span class="badge__state">@GitHubStateLabel(row.Snapshot?.Issue.State)</span>
                        </a>

                        @if (row.Snapshot?.Headline is { } pull)
                        {
                            <a class="badge badge--gh badge--gh-@(GitHubStateClass(pull.State))"
                               data-testid="github-pr-badge"
                               href="@pull.Url"
                               target="_blank"
                               rel="noopener"
                               title="@pull.Title">
                                PR #@pull.Number
                                <span class="badge__state">@GitHubStateLabel(pull.State)</span>
                            </a>
                        }
                    }
                }

                @if (!row.IsPersisted)
                {
                    <Badge Kind="draft" data-testid="draft-hint">Unsaved — give it a title line</Badge>
                }

                @* The size at a glance, so an estimate is visible on the entry
                   without opening the Ranking picker to read it. Only when it is
                   set: an entry nobody has sized wears no badge rather than one
                   saying "none", the same way an unset scheduling field shows
                   nothing on the row. *@
                @if (row.PreviewEffort is { } effort)
                {
                    <Badge Kind="effort" data-testid="entry-effort-badge" title="Effort">@FormatEffort(effort)</Badge>
                }

                @* Grouped and named after the entry, out loud. Both of these act on
                   the whole task — one files it as an issue, the other hands the
                   entry to a tool — and a step used to carry buttons that looked
                   identical, one of which quietly acted on the parent anyway. A
                   group whose label says which thing is being handed over is the
                   difference between an icon you press and an icon you trust. *@
                <span class="entry-doc__actions"
                      role="group"
                      aria-label="@($"Hand over the whole entry: {(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(row.PreviewTitle) ? "Untitled" : row.PreviewTitle)}")">
                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(BacklogFeatures.GitHubIntegration) && State.GitHubConfigured)
                    {
                        var repository = RepositoryFor(row);

                        @if (row.IssueLink is not null)
                        {
                            <IconButton BaseClass="entry-doc__icon-action"
                                        TestId="github-refresh-button"
                                        Disabled="@row.GitHubBusy"
                                        AriaLabel="Check this issue on GitHub"
                                        OnClick="() => State.RefreshGitHubAsync(row)">
                                @if (row.GitHubBusy)
                                {
                                    <span aria-hidden="true">…</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <svg class="entry-doc__button-icon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                                        <path d="M1.705 8a6.303 6.303 0 0 1 10.848-4.365L11.318 4.87a.75.75 0 0 0 .53 1.281h3.329a.75.75 0 0 0 .75-.75V2.073a.75.75 0 0 0-1.28-.53l-1.018 1.018A7.804 7.804 0 0 0 .204 8a.75.75 0 0 0 1.501 0Zm14.091 0a.75.75 0 0 0-1.501 0 6.303 6.303 0 0 1-10.848 4.365l1.235-1.235a.75.75 0 0 0-.53-1.281H.823a.75.75 0 0 0-.75.75v3.328a.75.75 0 0 0 1.28.53l1.018-1.018A7.804 7.804 0 0 0 15.796 8Z" />
                                    </svg>
                                }
                            </IconButton>
                        }
                        else if (repository is not null && row.IsPersisted)
                        {
                            <IconButton BaseClass="entry-doc__icon-action entry-doc__icon-action--push"
                                        TestId="github-push-button"
                                        Disabled="@row.GitHubBusy"
                                        AriaLabel="@($"Create an issue for this whole entry in {repository.FullName}")"
                                        OnClick="() => State.PushToGitHubAsync(row, repository)">
                                @if (row.GitHubBusy)
                                {
                                    <span aria-hidden="true">…</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <svg class="entry-doc__button-icon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                                        <path d="M8 9.5a1.5 1.5 0 1 0 0-3 1.5 1.5 0 0 0 0 3ZM8 0a8 8 0 1 1 0 16A8 8 0 0 1 8 0ZM1.5 8a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 13 0 6.5 6.5 0 0 0-13 0Z" />
                                    </svg>
                                }
                            </IconButton>
                        }
                    }

                    @if (IsFeatureEnabled(AppFeatureKeys.CopilotCli) && row.IsPersisted)
                    {
                        <IconButton BaseClass="entry-doc__icon-action entry-doc__icon-action--copilot"
                                    TestId="copilot-cli-button"
                                    Disabled="@row.CopilotBusy"
                                    AriaLabel="Start GitHub Copilot CLI for this whole entry"
                                    OnClick="() => State.StartCopilotCliAsync(row)">
                            @if (row.CopilotBusy)
                            {
                                <span aria-hidden="true">…</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <span class="entry-doc__cli-mark" aria-hidden="true">gh</span>
                            }
                        </IconButton>
                    }
                </span>
            </span>
        </div>;
    /// <summary>
    /// When the entry is due, when to be reminded of it, whether it comes back,
    /// whether it is in today's My Day, and what it waits on — the five facts
    /// <c>.domain/backlog/features.md#feature-scheduling-and-recurrence</c>,
    /// <c>#feature-my-day</c> and <c>#feature-entry-dependencies</c> add.
    ///
    /// <para>All five are still written by rewriting the metadata line, so typing
    /// <c>due:2026-08-21</c> into the raw hatch and pressing one of these are the
    /// same edit arriving by two routes. Nothing here is a second source of
    /// truth.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Every row is a TaskAction because all five are the same shape: a row
    /// that is set says what it is set to and offers an ✕, and a row that is not
    /// says what it would do. My Day is the one that toggles — pressing it again
    /// is how it comes off — so it deliberately has no ✕.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>In the detail pane rather than under the entry in the list, which is
    /// where they were and where a one-line entry could never reach them: which
    /// row is open no longer depends on how much it has been written in.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>The rows are laid out by <c>TaskActionPane</c> rather than stacked
    /// here, which is what changed when the pane adopted the shared panel. Two
    /// things follow from that and both are improvements rather than side effects.
    /// My Day is the lead row, across the top and carrying weight, because it is
    /// the one row here that is a decision somebody makes again every morning
    /// where the rest are facts about the entry that were settled once. And the
    /// facts are grouped and captioned instead of running down one column: a due
    /// date standing next to a dependency reads as related whether or not it is,
    /// and the reader picks the subject before the row.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Each picker is rendered inside the group of the row that opens it, so
    /// a date field appears under "Due date" and not at the bottom of the pane.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderEntrySchedule(EntryRow row) =>
        @<TaskActionPane CssClass="entry-doc__schedule" TestId="entry-schedule">
            <Lead>
                @* The decision rather than a fact, so the one row with a border and
                   the brand colour on it. Togglable, so it has no ✕: pressing it
                   again is how it comes off. *@
                <TaskAction Icon="☀"
                            Label="@(IsInMyDay(row) ? "Added to My Day" : "Add to My Day")"
                            Set="@IsInMyDay(row)"
                            Togglable="true"
                            Prominent="true"
                            OnSet="() => OnMyDayToggledAsync(row)"
                            TestId="entry-action-myday" />
            </Lead>
            <Groups>

                <TaskActionGroup Label="Scheduling" TestId="entry-schedule-scheduling">
                    <TaskAction Icon="⏰"
                                Label="Remind me"
                                Value="@FormatReminder(row.PreviewRemindAt)"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewRemindAt is not null)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Reminder)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeReminderAsync(row, null)"
                                TestId="entry-action-remind" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Reminder))
                    {
                        <TextField Bare="true"
                                   Type="datetime-local"
                                   AriaLabel="Reminder"
                                   InputCssClass="entry-doc__schedule-input"
                                   data-testid="entry-remind-input"
                                   Value="@(row.PreviewRemindAt is { } remindAt ? EntryTextParser.ReminderToken(remindAt) : string.Empty)"
                                   @onchange="(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnReminderPickedAsync(row, e)" />
                    }

                    <TaskAction Icon="🗓"
                                Label="Add due date"
                                Value="@FormatDate(row.PreviewDueOn)"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewDueOn is not null)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Due)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeDueAsync(row, null)"
                                TestId="entry-action-due" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Due))
                    {
                        @* TextField rather than a hand-rolled input: the library's
                           field grew date support for this, which is the deal the
                           shared-control rules ask for — give the component the hook,
                           do not ship a second implementation beside it.

                           @onchange rather than ValueChanged, because a half-typed
                           date raises input events reading as empty, and empty means
                           "clear the due date". A date commits when it is a date. *@
                        <TextField Bare="true"
                                   Type="date"
                                   AriaLabel="Due date"
                                   InputCssClass="entry-doc__schedule-input"
                                   data-testid="entry-due-input"
                                   Value="@(row.PreviewDueOn is { } due ? EntryTextParser.DateToken(due) : string.Empty)"
                                   @onchange="(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnDuePickedAsync(row, e)" />
                    }

                    <TaskAction Icon="🔁"
                                Label="Repeat"
                                Value="@FormatRepeat(row.PreviewRecurrence)"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewRecurrence is not null)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Repeat)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeRepeatAsync(row, null)"
                                TestId="entry-action-repeat" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Repeat))
                    {
                        @* The option values are the repeat tokens themselves, so what
                           the select offers and what the metadata line can hold cannot
                           drift apart — a shape with no spelling in the grammar has no
                           option here either. *@
                        <SelectField CurrentValue="@(row.PreviewRecurrence is { } repeat ? EntryTextParser.RepeatToken(repeat) : string.Empty)"
                                     Options="@RepeatOptions()"
                                     IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                                     EmptyLabel="Never"
                                     Label="Repeat"
                                     Id="@($"entry-repeat-{row.Key}")"
                                     TestId="entry-repeat-select"
                                     CssClass="entry-doc__schedule-select"
                                     OnChanged="value => OnRepeatPickedAsync(row, value)" />
                    }
                </TaskActionGroup>

                @* How much it matters, in a group of its own rather than a row among
                   the dates. A ranking and a deadline are the two facts most often
                   mistaken for each other, and the pane's job is to stop saying them
                   in the same breath: "critical" is how much this matters, "Friday"
                   is when it stops being possible. It was a badge on the line above
                   until the storybook's shape was adopted, where it read as one more
                   label rather than as the decision it is.

                   First of the two groups that follow "Scheduling", and that is a
                   layout fact rather than a claim about importance: TaskActionPane
                   flows its groups down one column and then into the next, so the
                   order they are written in is the order the second column reads.
                   Ranking's two rows and Attachments' one make a second column of
                   three, level with Scheduling's three in the first — which is what
                   a pane reads as one block rather than as a step. *@
                <TaskActionGroup Label="Ranking" TestId="entry-schedule-ranking">
                    @* Always set, and so no ✕. Every other row here can be absent,
                       but an entry has a priority whether or not anybody typed one
                       — the field is not nullable and `*medium` is what a silent
                       entry means. A clear button would offer to unset something
                       that has no unset state, and would write `*low` while
                       claiming to have removed a ranking. *@
                    <TaskAction Icon="⚑"
                                Label="Priority"
                                Value="@Humanize(row.PreviewPriority.ToString())"
                                Set="true"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Priority)"
                                TestId="entry-action-priority" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Priority))
                    {
                        <SelectField CurrentValue="@row.PreviewPriority.ToString()"
                                     Options="@PrioritySelectorOptions()"
                                     Label="Priority"
                                     Id="@($"entry-priority-{row.Key}")"
                                     TestId="entry-priority-select"
                                     CssClass="entry-doc__schedule-select"
                                     OnChanged="value => OnPriorityPickedAsync(row, value)" />
                    }

                    @* Unlike priority this one can be absent, and so it carries a ✕:
                       "not estimated" is a real state an entry starts in and comes
                       back to, where a priority is always something. The presets are
                       the Fibonacci points estimation conventionally uses; the field
                       itself holds any non-negative number a person or an agent
                       writes into the text, so the picker is a convenience over the
                       token rather than the limit of it. *@
                    <TaskAction Icon="📐"
                                Label="Effort"
                                Value="@FormatEffort(row.PreviewEffort)"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewEffort is not null)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Effort)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeEffortAsync(row, null)"
                                TestId="entry-action-effort" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Effort))
                    {
                        <SelectField CurrentValue="@(row.PreviewEffort is { } effort ? effort.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) : string.Empty)"
                                     Options="@EffortSelectorOptions()"
                                     IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                                     EmptyLabel="Not estimated"
                                     Label="Effort"
                                     Id="@($"entry-effort-{row.Key}")"
                                     TestId="entry-effort-select"
                                     CssClass="entry-doc__schedule-select"
                                     OnChanged="value => OnEffortPickedAsync(row, value)" />
                    }
                </TaskActionGroup>

                @* One row pointing at one place, and never a row per file. What is
                   attached is a folder or an archive; the row says which and what it
                   is called. A row each would make the pane's height a function of
                   how many files somebody dropped on the entry, and the height of a
                   side panel is the one measurement everything else has to live with
                   — every row it grows is a row the body loses. *@
                <TaskActionGroup Label="Attachments" TestId="entry-schedule-attachments">
                    <TaskAction Icon="📁"
                                Label="@(row.PreviewAttachment is { } attached ? (attached.IsArchive ? "Archive" : "Folder") : "Attach a folder or zip")"
                                Value="@row.PreviewAttachment?.Name"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewAttachment is not null)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.Attachment)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeAttachmentAsync(row, null)"
                                TestId="entry-action-files" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.Attachment))
                    {
                        @* A path, typed or pasted. There is no file dialog behind
                           this and there deliberately is not one yet: the desktop
                           channel could open one and the phone and the IDE could
                           not, and a control that only exists on one head is a
                           control the grammar cannot promise. The token is a path,
                           so a path is what this asks for.

                           @onchange rather than ValueChanged, because a half-typed
                           path raises input events and every one of them would be a
                           save — and an empty one means "detach". A path commits
                           when the reader is done with it. *@
                        <TextField Bare="true"
                                   AriaLabel="Path to a folder or zip"
                                   Placeholder="C:\folder, or a .zip"
                                   InputCssClass="entry-doc__schedule-input"
                                   data-testid="entry-files-input"
                                   Value="@(row.PreviewAttachment?.Path ?? string.Empty)"
                                   @onchange="(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnAttachmentPickedAsync(row, e)" />
                    }
                </TaskActionGroup>

                @* The star is still deliberately absent. TaskRow carries an
                   Important flag and a backlog entry has no importance field, so a
                   control here would be inventing domain state: the flag would go
                   nowhere and the reader would think they had said something. *@
            </Groups>
            <Trailing>
                @* Its own group rather than a fourth row under "Scheduling". What an
                   entry waits on is not when it happens: a chain is about the other
                   entries, and standing it beside a due date would read as a
                   deadline it is not.

                   Under the columns rather than in them, because what it is set to
                   is a list of other entries and a column is about sixteen rem wide.
                   In a column the picker's chips wrapped one per line and the row
                   became a paragraph; across the full width they read as the row
                   they are. It also leaves the columns three rows each, which is
                   what makes them end level with one another. *@
                <TaskActionGroup Label="Dependencies" TestId="entry-schedule-dependencies">
                    <TaskAction Icon="⏳"
                                Label="Waiting for"
                                Value="@FormatDependencies(row)"
                                Set="@(row.PreviewDependsOn.Count > 0)"
                                OnSet="() => TogglePicker(SchedulePicker.DependsOn)"
                                OnClear="() => State.ChangeDependsOnAsync(row, [])"
                                TestId="entry-action-depends" />

                    @if (IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker.DependsOn))
                    {
                        @* The library ships no dependency editor and is not going to:
                           a chain is data the host owns, so the picker is built here
                           out of TagMultiSelect, its options the other entries in view
                           and its chips this entry's own ids. *@
                        <TagMultiSelect Options="@DependencyOptions(row)"
                                        SelectedValues="@row.PreviewDependsOn"
                                        SelectedValuesChanged="ids => State.ChangeDependsOnAsync(row, ids)"
                                        Placeholder="Add an entry"
                                        CssClass="entry-doc__schedule-depends"
                                        TestId="entry-depends-select" />
                    }
                </TaskActionGroup>
            </Trailing>
        </TaskActionPane>;
    /// <summary>
    /// The entry's tags, as chips you can drop and a field you can type a new one
    /// into.
    /// <para>
    /// <c>TagMultiSelect</c> rather than the line of space-separated text this used
    /// to be, and the reason is the panel it now sits in: <c>TaskPanel</c> draws
    /// tags as <c>TagChip</c>s because that is what a tag looks like everywhere in
    /// the product, and a strip of chips beside a field spelling the same tags out
    /// again would be the same fact said twice in two shapes. This is the one
    /// control that is both — the chips are the reading and the removing, the field
    /// is the adding, and the popup offers every tag already in use.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>AllowCreate</c>, because a tag is whatever somebody typed. The options are
    /// suggestions and never the whole vocabulary, which is exactly what the
    /// datalist behind the old field was.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The panel's own <c>Tags</c> parameter is deliberately left unset. Its strip
    /// reads tags; this entry's are edited, and a control is not something a
    /// read-only strip can stand in for.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderTagEditor(EntryRow row) =>
        @<TagMultiSelect Options="@TagSelectorOptions(row)"
                         SelectedValues="@row.PreviewMetadataTags"
                         SelectedValuesChanged="tags => State.ChangeTagsAsync(row, tags)"
                         AllowCreate="true"
                         Placeholder="Tags"
                         CssClass="entry-doc__tag-picker"
                         TestId="entry-tags-input" />;
    // --- Keyboard ----------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// The shortcut `#raw-markdown-escape-hatch` asks for: Ctrl+Shift+M shows or
    /// hides the canonical markdown. On the pane rather than on the toggle, because
    /// a shortcut that only worked while its own button had focus would be a
    /// shortcut for people who had already found the button.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnDetailKeyDownAsync(EntryRow row, KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key is "Escape")
        {
            // The source first: with the hatch open, Escape is about the hatch.
            // Closing the whole pane from under a reader who asked to leave one
            // control would take two decisions out of one keystroke.
            if (State.RawHatchOpen)
            {
                await State.ToggleRawHatchAsync();
                return;
            }

            await CloseToListAsync();
            return;
        }

        if (e.CtrlKey && e.ShiftKey && e.Key is "M" or "m") await State.ToggleRawHatchAsync();
    }

    // --- Focus between the two halves --------------------------------------
    //
    // In reading order the pane comes after the whole list, so plain Tab from the
    // open row lands on the next row and the pane is only reachable after every
    // remaining one. These three are the shortcut across, the way back, and the
    // rule that decides when the pane goes away.

    /// <summary>Tab off the open row moves into the pane beside it.</summary>
    private async Task MoveFocusToDetailAsync()
    {
        if (State.SelectedRow is null || _panel is null) return;

        await _panel.FocusFirstAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Shift+Tab off the front of the pane moves back to the open row.
    /// The same move Escape makes, minus the closing.</summary>
    private async Task MoveFocusToListAsync()
    {
        if (_list is null) return;

        await _list.FocusSelectedAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Escape: back to the row, then closed. In that order, because the
    /// list is asked to focus the row it still has selected — clearing first would
    /// leave nothing to aim at and the focus on a pane that is no longer
    /// there.</summary>
    private async Task CloseToListAsync()
    {
        await MoveFocusToListAsync();
        await State.SelectAsync(null);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The focus left both halves, so nothing is open any more.
    /// <para>
    /// Which is the same thing as the pane going away, and the reason it is
    /// focusout on the region containing both rather than a blur on either: moving
    /// from the row into the pane is a move <em>within</em> the region and must not
    /// clear anything, and neither is picking a status chip or dragging the
    /// separator.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Where the focus went is asked of the document afterwards rather than read
    /// off the event, because Blazor's <c>FocusEventArgs</c> has no
    /// <c>relatedTarget</c>: it carries the event type and nothing else. The
    /// question is also asked the strict way round — did the focus land on
    /// something else — so that a focus which landed nowhere, as it does when a
    /// closing date picker takes the focused element with it, is not read as the
    /// reader having moved on.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A failed question leaves the selection alone. An unanswerable "did they
    /// leave?" is not a yes, and closing the pane somebody is using is the more
    /// expensive way to be wrong.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnPaneFocusOutAsync()
    {
        if (State.SelectedRow is not { } openedRow) return;

        bool left;

        try
        {
            left = await JS.InvokeAsync<bool>("backlogFocusOutside", DetailBoundarySelectors);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            return;
        }

        if (!left) return;

        // Picking a different row is a switch, not a departure — and by the time
        // the JS round trip above resolves, that click's own selection may already
        // have landed. Closing on top of it would hide the row the reader just
        // opened rather than the one this check was ever asking about.
        if (!ReferenceEquals(State.SelectedRow, openedRow)) return;

        await State.SelectAsync(null);
    }

    // --- Writes ------------------------------------------------------------

    private async Task OnDeleteTaskAsync(EntryRow row) => await State.DeleteRowAsync(row);

    private async Task OnTypeChangedAsync(EntryRow row, string? value)
    {
        if (Enum.TryParse<EntryType>(value, out var type))
        {
            await State.ChangeTypeAsync(row, type);
        }
    }

    private async Task OnRepositoryChangedAsync(EntryRow row, string? repositoryAlias) =>
        await State.ChangeAreaAsync(row, repositoryAlias);

    private async Task OnStatusChangedAsync(EntryRow row, string value)
    {
        if (Enum.TryParse<EntryStatus>(value, out var status))
        {
            await State.ChangeStatusAsync(row, status);
        }
    }

    // The scheduling and dependency controls. Reading a clock is the pane's job
    // rather than the state's or the row's: My Day expires by arithmetic against
    // "the reader's current local date", and there is exactly one place here that
    // knows what that is.

    private static DateOnly Today => DateOnly.FromDateTime(DateTime.Now);

    /// <summary>An entry is in My Day exactly while its stamp is today's local
    /// date. A stamp from yesterday is not "in My Day yesterday" — it is simply
    /// not in My Day, which is what lets the list clear itself overnight with no
    /// timer and nothing to sweep.</summary>
    private static bool IsInMyDay(EntryRow row) => row.PreviewInMyDayOn == Today;

    private async Task OnMyDayToggledAsync(EntryRow row)
    {
        // Off is clearing the stamp; on is stamping today. Never "today minus
        // one" — there is no way to be in yesterday's My Day.
        await State.ChangeMyDayAsync(row, IsInMyDay(row) ? null : Today);
    }

    private bool IsPickerOpen(SchedulePicker kind) => _openPicker == kind;

    /// <summary>Opens the picker a TaskAction stands for, or closes it if it was
    /// already the open one — pressing the row twice puts it back.</summary>
    private void TogglePicker(SchedulePicker kind) =>
        _openPicker = IsPickerOpen(kind) ? null : kind;

    private void ClosePicker() => _openPicker = null;

    private async Task OnDuePickedAsync(EntryRow row, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        var value = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;
        ClosePicker();

        // Clearing the native field is clearing the date, which is the same
        // gesture as the ✕ on the row above it.
        await State.ChangeDueAsync(
            row,
            DateOnly.TryParseExact(value, "yyyy-MM-dd", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, DateTimeStyles.None, out var due)
                ? due
                : null);
    }

    private async Task OnReminderPickedAsync(EntryRow row, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        var value = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;
        ClosePicker();

        // No AssumeLocal and no AdjustToUniversal: a reminder is wall-clock
        // intent, and the Unspecified kind this leaves behind is the whole point.
        await State.ChangeReminderAsync(
            row,
            DateTime.TryParseExact(
                value,
                ["yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm", "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss"],
                CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                DateTimeStyles.None,
                out var remindAt)
                ? remindAt
                : null);
    }

    private async Task OnRepeatPickedAsync(EntryRow row, string? value)
    {
        ClosePicker();
        await State.ChangeRepeatAsync(row, EntryTextParser.ParseRepeat(value));
    }

    /// <summary>A ranking that does not parse is nothing being asked for, so
    /// nothing is written — unlike the fields around it there is no "clear" to fall
    /// back to, because an entry always has a priority.</summary>
    private async Task OnPriorityPickedAsync(EntryRow row, string? value)
    {
        ClosePicker();

        if (Enum.TryParse<Priority>(value, out var priority))
        {
            await State.ChangePriorityAsync(row, priority);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Estimates the entry, or clears the estimate when the empty option
    /// is chosen. A value that is not one of the offered points is nothing being
    /// asked for and writes nothing — but unlike priority the empty option is a
    /// real answer here, because "not estimated" is a state the entry can be in and
    /// the ✕ and this option are the two ways back to it.</summary>
    private async Task OnEffortPickedAsync(EntryRow row, string? value)
    {
        ClosePicker();

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value))
        {
            await State.ChangeEffortAsync(row, null);
            return;
        }

        if (int.TryParse(value, NumberStyles.None, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var points))
        {
            await State.ChangeEffortAsync(row, points);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Attaches whatever path was typed, and detaches on an empty one —
    /// clearing the field is the same gesture as the ✕ on the row above it.</summary>
    private async Task OnAttachmentPickedAsync(EntryRow row, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        ClosePicker();
        await State.ChangeAttachmentAsync(row, e.Value?.ToString());
    }

    /// <summary>The repeat shapes this grammar can write down, which is
    /// deliberately the same list as the shapes it can read back.</summary>
    private static IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> RepeatOptions() =>
    [
        new("daily", "Daily"),
        new("weekly", "Weekly"),
        new("weekdays", "Every weekday"),
        new("monthly", "Monthly"),
        new("yearly", "Yearly")
    ];

    /// <summary>Every other entry in view, by id and title. Its own row is left
    /// out: an entry that waits on itself is a cycle of one, and while the model
    /// names cycles rather than breaking them there is no reason to make this one
    /// a click away.
    /// <para>
    /// Repository-authored rows are left out too, for the same reason no picker is
    /// offered on one: with no id there is nothing an <c>after:</c> token could
    /// name them by.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Done rows are left out too. A finished predecessor cannot block, so
    /// offering one would let a reader write down a wait that TaskChain silently
    /// drops the moment it resolves the chain — the picker would look like it
    /// worked and the list row would look like it did not.
    /// </para></summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> DependencyOptions(EntryRow row) =>
        [.. State.FilteredRows
            .Where(candidate => !ReferenceEquals(candidate, row)
                && candidate.Id is not null
                && candidate.PreviewStatus is not EntryStatus.Done)
            .Select(candidate => new SelectorOption(
                candidate.Id!.Value.ToString(),
                string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(candidate.PreviewTitle) ? "Untitled" : candidate.PreviewTitle))];

    /// <summary>What the entry is waiting for, said by title. An id naming
    /// nothing in view is shown as the id rather than dropped — it still blocks,
    /// and a waiting line that quietly omitted it would read as a shorter wait
    /// than the entry actually has.</summary>
    private string FormatDependencies(EntryRow row) =>
        string.Join(", ", row.PreviewDependsOn.Select(DependencyLabel));

    private string DependencyLabel(string id) =>
        State.Rows.FirstOrDefault(candidate =>
            candidate.Id is { } candidateId
            && string.Equals(candidateId.ToString(), id, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) is { } match
            ? (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(match.PreviewTitle) ? "Untitled" : match.PreviewTitle)
            : id;

    // What a date is called, and in what language, belongs to whatever is showing
    // it — TaskAction and TaskRow both take an already-formatted string precisely
    // so a component never has a culture in it. The token forms stay invariant;
    // only these do not.

    private static string? FormatDate(DateOnly? date) =>
        date?.ToString("d", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    private static string? FormatReminder(DateTime? remindAt) =>
        remindAt?.ToString("g", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    /// <summary>A repeat said in words. Named shapes get their name because
    /// "Weekly" is what a person would have said; an interval falls back to
    /// counting, which is all there is to say about "every 3 weeks".</summary>
    private static string? FormatRepeat(Recurrence? recurrence) => recurrence switch
    {
        null => null,
        { Weekdays.Count: > 0 } => EntryTextParser.RepeatToken(recurrence) == "weekdays"
            ? "Every weekday"
            : IntervalWords(recurrence),
        { Interval: 1, Unit: RecurrenceUnit.Day } => "Daily",
        { Interval: 1, Unit: RecurrenceUnit.Week } => "Weekly",
        { Interval: 1, Unit: RecurrenceUnit.Month } => "Monthly",
        { Interval: 1, Unit: RecurrenceUnit.Year } => "Yearly",
        _ => IntervalWords(recurrence)
    };

    private static string IntervalWords(Recurrence recurrence) =>
        $"Every {recurrence.Interval.ToString(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)} {recurrence.Unit.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()}s";

    private static IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> TypeSelectorOptions() =>
        [.. Enum.GetValues<EntryType>().Select(type => new SelectorOption(type.ToString(), Humanize(type.ToString())))];

    /// <summary>All four rankings the domain has, lowest first. The badge this
    /// replaced offered three — Critical was in the enum, in the grammar and in the
    /// storybook, and unreachable from the pane.</summary>
    private static IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> PrioritySelectorOptions() =>
        [.. Enum.GetValues<Priority>().Select(priority => new SelectorOption(priority.ToString(), Humanize(priority.ToString())))];

    /// <summary>The Fibonacci points the picker offers — the scale story-point
    /// estimation conventionally uses, where the gaps widen as the numbers grow so
    /// a larger estimate is also a coarser one. The value is the plain number, the
    /// same token the metadata line holds; the field accepts any non-negative
    /// number typed into the text, so these are the presets the picker speaks
    /// rather than the limit of what round-trips.</summary>
    private static IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> EffortSelectorOptions() =>
        [.. new[] { 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21 }.Select(points => new SelectorOption(
            points.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture),
            points.ToString(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)))];

    /// <summary>A size said in words for the row and the badge — "5 points", and
    /// "1 point" rather than "1 points". Null when nothing is estimated, which is
    /// what leaves the badge off and the action reading "Effort" rather than a
    /// number. What the count is called is the host's the same way a date's
    /// wording is; only the token form stays invariant.</summary>
    private static string? FormatEffort(int? effort) => effort is { } points
        ? $"{points.ToString(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)} {(points == 1 ? "point" : "points")}"
        : null;

    private static IReadOnlyList<EntryStatus> StatusOptions() => Enum.GetValues<EntryStatus>();

    private static IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> StatusSelectorOptions() =>
        [.. StatusOptions().Select(status => new SelectorOption(status.ToString(), Humanize(status.ToString())))];

    private IReadOnlyList<string> TagOptions(EntryRow row) =>
        BacklogTags(row)
            .Concat(State.RoadmapTags)
            .Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            .OrderBy(tag => tag, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            .ToList();

    /// <summary>The tags the backlog itself is already using — every entry's, and the
    /// one being edited. The roadmap's are unioned onto these; kept apart so an option
    /// can be told which side it came from.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<string> BacklogTags(EntryRow row) =>
        State.Rows
            .SelectMany(existing => existing.PreviewMetadataTags)
            .Concat(row.PreviewMetadataTags);

    /// <summary>Every tag on offer, as the picker wants them. A tag is its own label,
    /// so the two halves of the option are the same string — which is what makes a tag
    /// typed here and a tag picked here the same value. A tag the roadmap offers that
    /// no entry uses yet carries a hint saying where it came from, so an option nothing
    /// in the list explains does not read as a stray.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> TagSelectorOptions(EntryRow row)
    {
        var used = BacklogTags(row).ToHashSet(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

        return
        [
            .. TagOptions(row).Select(tag => new SelectorOption(
                tag,
                tag,
                used.Contains(tag) ? null : "from roadmap"))
        ];
    }

    private static string Humanize(string enumName)
    {
        var text = System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Replace(enumName, "(?<!^)([A-Z])", " $1");
        return text.ToLowerInvariant();
    }

    /// <summary>"never checked" is a real, distinct answer from "open" — the
    /// badge says so rather than implying a state nobody has verified.</summary>
    private static string GitHubStateLabel(GitHubItemState? state) => state switch
    {
        GitHubItemState.Open => "open",
        GitHubItemState.Draft => "draft",
        GitHubItemState.Merged => "merged",
        GitHubItemState.Closed => "closed",
        _ => "not checked"
    };

    private static string GitHubStateClass(GitHubItemState? state) =>
        state?.ToString().ToLowerInvariant() ?? "unknown";

    public void Dispose()
    {
        State.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;
        Features.Changed -= HandleFeaturesChanged;
    }
}
